EXT. DUSTY CROSSROADS - FOOD TRUCK - DAY

A weathered truck painted "BEANS & BRISKET - SINCE YESTERDAY" squats in the sun. Heat shimmers. A line of two teenagers in cowboy hats waits at the window while a mangy dog sleeps under the awning.

INSIDE, SADIE KROLL, 40s, cook, jaw like a knuckle, slaps patties on the flat-top. Her brother-in-law CLETUS, 50s, leans on the counter pretending to count money. At the window, a stranger in a long duster, MARSHAL REEVES, 60s, sets down his hat. And in the corner, her teenage niece JOLENE, 17, wipes a spoon and watches everything.

CLOSE ON Reeves's badge, half hidden under the duster.

REEVES
Two plates. And a word with Cletus.

CLETUS
(laughing too loud)
Marshal! Long way from town.

SADIE
(not turning from the grill)
He's not here for beans, Cletus.

The dog LIFTS its head. A screen door SLAMS in the distance.

REEVES
(quietly)
Where's the payroll box, Cletus?

ANGLE ON Jolene's hand sliding a cleaver off the shelf.

JOLENE
Uncle Cletus, he's got a warrant folded in his boot.

CLETUS
(whispering)
Jolene, put that down.

SADIE
(spinning around with a spatula held like a pistol)
Nobody's cutting anybody. Marshal, sit. Eat. Then we'll talk.

REEVES
(tipping his hat back, almost smiling)
Sadie, you've never once made it easy.

BACK TO SCENE: the two teenagers bolt, the dog barks, a plate of brisket hits the dirt, and somewhere in the truck a timer DINGS.
